[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  friday july 29th i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  facebook parent meta has been trying to  weather storm after storm lately  economic conditions like rising  inflation are weighing on advertising  revenue competitors are becoming an  increasing threat for users attention  meta's leadership is changing and  regulators have their eyes trained on  the company  and those are just some of the most  recent struggles  on the company's earnings call on  wednesday ceo mark zuckerberg tried to  reassure investors that meta was  prepared for the headwinds  we're focused on making the long-term  investments that will position us to be  stronger coming out of this downturn  including our work on our discovery  engine and reels our new ads  infrastructure and  the metaverse  and we're also focused on being rigorous  about measuring returns and sizing these  investments correctly but it's unclear  how successful those reassurances have  really been  meta shares are down and facebook and  instagram users who are experiencing  changes to the platforms have been  voicing their displeasure  joining us to discuss all of this is our  reporter salvador rodriguez  hey sal thanks for being here  yeah thanks for having me as always  so we heard a clip from mark zuckerberg  talking about the company you know  wanting to focus on their discovery  engine and reels you know those changes  to the platforms but also their ad  infrastructure and the metaverse so  lay this out for us what kind of  situation has the company found itself  in and why are these the areas that it's  focusing on  so meta is basically in a position that  it has been in twice before in its  history and that is that it is having to  basically reinvent and very much pivot  its company for the sake of going  through you know an existential threat  we saw it in 2012 when the company was  getting ready to ipo  at that time social media was very much  shifting from being  web-based desktop-based to mobile first  and the company  really had to revamp its mobile apps and  spend a billion dollars acquiring  instagram we saw it again in the period  between 2016 and 2018  when they had to  essentially copy snapchat's signature  feature stories  and they launched that on instagram and  later the rest of their apps and after  that they had to monetize that feature  to really just kind of  go to where the puck was going  and now we're seeing it happen yet again  and this time it's with tick tock and  with short form video but on top of all  that the company is also dealing with a  number of other macroeconomic factors  that are making the situation  much more difficult that of course being  inflation  you know the impact of apple's privacy  changes to facebook's advertising  business you know the looming recession  all those things are creating a really  difficult you can even call it a perfect  storm type situation for meta  so how is meta saying it's dealing with  these challenges  yeah so it's trying to do a number of  things to overcome this  it's long-term solution is to  build out the metaverse which is really  just a buzzword for  virtual reality this is something that  they've been working on for a while and  it's a key priority for them they want  people to have vr headsets and use that  as one of their main forms of computing  the reason for that is because  what meta lacks is its own hardware  device and when you don't have that as a  tech company you're always at the mercy  of someone else so they really want  virtual reality to take off and that's  their solution for the long term in the  short term they still need to get their  advertising business back in order and  they need to ensure that they don't  lose users to tick-tock so what they're  doing is essentially building out reels  which is essentially their version of  tick-tock  and not only are they building it out  but they're really emphasizing it  yeah let's talk about that a bit because  adam moseri the head of instagram  addressed some of the complaints about  changes the platform has made recently  including the increasing number of  videos and changing the kinds of posts  that users see he addressed this in a  video on tuesday let's take a listen  now i want to be clear we're going to  continue to support photos it's part of  our heritage i love photos i know a lot  of you out there love photos too that  said i need to be honest i do believe  that more and more of instagram is going  to become video over time  so that video was posted on tuesday  flash forward to thursday and missouri  is saying the company is walking back  some of those changes what did he  announce yeah so missouri in an  interview that was published on thursday  with platformer he said that the company  is actually going to roll back and  rethink some of these changes  the two that are getting rolled back is  a redesign that was being tested where  users were going to see photos and  videos in full screen so very similar to  the design of tick tock and the other  thing that is going to be rolled back to  a certain extent is the number of posts  that users see  based on ai recommendations from  accounts that they don't already follow  so these were two of the biggest changes  that they had announced this year and it  seems like instagram is really  rethinking just how much it can throw at  its users all at once  but what was made clear by misery in  this interview with platformer is that  these rollbacks are only temporary so  instagram users should still expect that  these changes will come down at some  point down the road  what does this mean in terms of their  efforts to get the the creator economy  and we heard you know some famous people  kylie jenner kim kardashian say they  they didn't like the changes that were  being implemented how do we expect that  to to impact instagram's decisions  yeah well in this interview that  missouri did with platformer he very  much addressed creators and talked about  how creators expect something stable you  know if they're gonna make your platform  their livelihood they need to understand  how it works have an understanding or an  expectation of which of their posts will  succeed and therefore be able to make an  income from this my favorite quote from  his interview with platformer  he said change is hard i also want to  acknowledge if you spend an hour at your  desk every day talking to your friends  and reading or watching tv and then i  come by and i just rearranged everything  without telling you you'd be pretty  pissed so i think we need to do a better  job making sure it's clear why something  is better in explaining something before  it happens so we don't surprise people  and i think that tells you everything  that you need to know where missouri and  the rest of meta they have so many  stakeholders that they need to answer to  whether it's your everyday user  you know creators or  trying to attract younger users who are  going off to tick tock and that's the  problem when your user base gets as big  as it is you have a lot of people that  you need to make happy and it's you know  it's it's very hard to do that  all right that's our reporter salvador  rodriguez thanks so much for joining us  sal thanks for having me  and that's it for tech news briefing  this week our producer is julie chang we  had editorial support from scott  salloway our supervising producer is  chris sinsley our executive producer is  kateri yocum and i'm your host zoe  thomas thanks for listening and have a  great weekend  [Music]  [Music]  you

